    **Ada — recovery of the argument (docs/passage.md)**
    Premise 1 — To cross any distance, a traveller must first cross half of it.
    Premise 2 — After that, half of what remains, and again half of what remains, without end.
    Premise 3 — A task made of endlessly many steps, each requiring some time, can never be completed.
    Conclusion (4) — Therefore the traveller never arrives: motion across a distance is impossible.
    Structure: 1 and 2 together make crossing an endless sequence of half-steps; 3 rules out ever finishing such a sequence; 4 follows that arrival — hence motion — is impossible. The load-bearing premise is 3: it quietly assumes that infinitely many steps must take infinite time, which is exactly where Ash's numerical test should press.

